# Line endings for executable scripts.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# core.autocrlf is true on the dev machine and this repo had no .gitattributes, so Git
# would write CRLF into every .sh on the next checkout or clone. A shell script with CRLF
# dies at the shebang ("bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M"), which would take out all
# 28 Claude Code hooks at once, silently: every hook fails open, so every gate turns off
# and nothing reports an error. The hooks are LF today only because tools wrote them
# directly rather than checking them out. `git diff` was already warning "LF will be
# replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" on all 15 modified hooks.
#
# Verify with: git ls-files --eol .claude/hooks/   (want i/lf w/lf on every row)
# Verified 2026-08-31: every tracked *.sh is already i/lf w/lf, so this pins the current
# state rather than rewriting anything. No churn.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# NOT *.py, deliberately. 72 tracked .py files are CRLF in the worktree; normalising them
# would rewrite all 72 on the next checkout for no benefit, since Python is CRLF-tolerant
# and no hook execs a .py through a shebang. If that churn is ever wanted, do it as its
# own commit with `git add --renormalize`, not as a side effect of a hook fix.
